  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nThis Afternoon - Tonight: Rapidly Increasing Rainfall Potential\n\nThe one big change to the near-term forecast has been a rapid \nincrease in the probabilities for light to moderate rainfall \nspreading eastward from central MN after noon, reaching the \nMississippi River towards sunset and lingering for many locales \nthrough the night into Friday. \n\nA small eastward wobble of the lower tropospheric ridge axis is\nall it took for the eastward extent of the rain shield to shift\nfrom the I-35 corridor to now western and central WI. The \nsignal in the short and medium range ensembles \n(LREF/EPS/GEFS/HREF) is nearly unanimous in there being rain \nwest of the Mississippi River, with the number of dry members \nincreasing as one heads further east. While the rainfall \nprobabilities are quite high, the intrusion of dry air from \n850-700-mb will limit rainfall amounts with the probabilities \nfor >0.25\" west of the Mississippi River only 10-20% and less \nthan 10% elsewhere. \n\nFriday - Saturday\n\nWeak 290K isentropic ascent and broad lower tropospheric \nconfluence look to keep the risk for showers going west of the \nMississippi River through Friday--again with minimal rainfall \namounts. How the rain on Saturday unfolds is of somewhat lesser\nconfidence with there likely being a sharp cutoff in the \neastward extent of the rain between I-35 and the Mississippi \nRiver--exactly where is to be determined. Once again, in the \nabsence of deep moisture or lift, rainfall amounts should be \nminimal with a 20-50% chance of >0.25\" and 10-20% chance of \n>0.50\" west of the Mississippi River. Upper tropospheric ridging\nbuilds to the west for Sunday and Monday, bringing a reprieve \nfrom the light rain to start the new week.\n\nTuesday - Wednesday: Next Widespread Rain Risk\n\nA longwave trough will move east through the Mid- and Upper \nMississippi River Valley. This will bring rain into the region. \nThere are some models that bring tropical moisture northward \ninto the region. Meanwhile, other models show that this plume of\ntropical moisture will be southeast of the area.\n\nHigh temperatures will be in the mid- to upper 60s. Low \ntemperatures will range from the mid-40s to mid-50s.",
